<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=assembly-line">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assembly line</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1104</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Production line, Progressive assembly</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An assembly line is a manufacturing process in which the unfinished product moves in a direct line from workstation to workstation, with parts added in sequence until it is complete. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetian-arsenal">Venetian Arsenal</a>, dating to about 1104, operated similarly to a production line, with ships moving down a canal and fitted by the shops they passed. The first flow assembly line was at Richard Garrett and Sons in Leiston, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>, fully operational by early 1853. Ransom Olds patented the concept in 1901, and the moving assembly line for the Ford Model T began on 7 October 1913 at Highland Park, cutting production time to 93 minutes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assembly-line.jpg" alt="Assembly line" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DearEdward from New York, NY, USA · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A321_final_assembly_(9351765668).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Highland Park, Michigan, Ford began building the Model T on a moving <b>assembly line</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Model T and moving <b>assembly line</b> made cars affordable to millions.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-16">16 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 1104</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Venice</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The Garrett works at Leiston (1853) is called the first flow assembly line and the Portsmouth Block Mills (1801 to 1803) probably the earliest linear continuous assembly process of the industrial age; production line methods did not become common after the Arsenal. Credit for the modern line is divided among Ransom Olds (1901) and Ford's team (1913).</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/interchangeable-parts">Interchangeable parts<span>264–241 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=assembly-line">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
